# Mind and society, stages 0 to 2 (ages 3 to 13).
# Name it, sort it, give a reason, and try the idea on a new case.

@ mind.0.feelings
goal: Name feelings, and notice how other people are feeling.
intro: Everyone has feelings. You can feel happy, sad, angry or scared. Faces and bodies show how people feel.
  show: emoji 😀 😢 😠 😨
demo: Look at a face. Is the mouth turned up? That person may be happy. Are there tears? That person may be sad. We can look, and we can ask.
  show: flow look at the face > think how they feel > ask "Are you all right?"
M: Match each face to the feeling.
  pairs: 😀 => Happy | 😢 => Sad | 😠 => Angry | 😨 => Scared
  hint: Which face has tears?
  why: A smile shows happy, tears show sad, a frown shows angry and wide eyes show scared.
G: What might make you feel happy? What might make you feel sad?
  bin Happy: a hug | playing with a friend | a birthday
  bin Sad: a broken toy | saying goodbye | a lost teddy
  hint: Think about how you would feel.
  why: Nice things can make us happy. Losing things or saying goodbye can make us sad.
P: Your friend is crying. How does your friend feel?
  + Sad :: Tears often mean that someone is sad.
  - Happy :: [misread-the-sign] Happy people usually smile.
  - Sleepy :: [misread-the-sign] Sleepy people yawn. Crying is a sign of being sad.
  hint: When do people cry?
  why: Crying is one way that people show they are sad.
>P: Someone knocks down Sam's tower. Sam's face goes red and his hands make fists. How does Sam feel, and what could help?
  + Angry. He can take slow breaths. :: Slow breaths help angry feelings to calm down.
  - Happy. He can laugh. :: [misread-the-sign] A red face and fists are signs of being angry.
  - Sleepy. He can go to bed. :: [misread-the-sign] Sam is not tired. He is cross about his tower.
  hint: How would you feel if your tower was knocked down?
  why: A red face and tight fists show anger. Breathing slowly, or asking for help, makes it easier to calm down.
done: All feelings are all right to have. Tell someone how you feel today.

@ mind.0.fair
goal: Share, take turns and be kind.
intro: Being fair means that everyone gets a turn and everyone gets a share. Being kind means doing things that help other people feel good.
  show: emoji 🤝 💛
demo: There is one ball and there are two children. Mia has a turn. Then Tom has a turn. Now both have played. That is fair.
  show: flow Mia has a turn > Tom has a turn > both have played
G: Kind or unkind?
  bin Kind: sharing your toys | saying thank you | helping to tidy up
  bin Unkind: pushing | grabbing | calling someone names
  hint: Would it make the other person feel good or bad?
  why: Kind things help others to feel good. Unkind things hurt.
P: There is one swing and there are two children. What is fair?
  + They take turns :: Then both have a go.
  - The bigger child has it all day :: [biggest-wins] Being bigger does not make it fair.
  - Nobody uses it :: [gave-up] Taking turns lets both of them play.
  hint: How can both children have a go?
  why: Taking turns is fair, because everyone gets a go.
F: Share the biscuits fairly.
  frame: There are 4 biscuits and 2 children. Each child gets __
  taps: 1 | 2 | 4
  answer: 2
  x 4 :: [one-gets-all] If one child has all 4, the other has none.
  x 1 :: [left-some-over] With 1 each, 2 biscuits are left over. Share them all.
  hint: Give one to each child, then one more to each.
  why: 2 for one child and 2 for the other. Both have the same.
>P: A new child is standing all alone at playtime. What is the kind thing to do?
  + Ask them to play :: Then they are not alone.
  - Laugh at them :: [unkind-choice] That would hurt their feelings.
  - Walk away :: [ignored-them] They would still be alone.
  hint: How would you feel if you were all alone?
  why: Being kind means noticing when someone needs a friend.
done: Share, take turns, be kind. Do one kind thing today.

@ mind.0.choices
goal: Make a choice, and say why you made it.
intro: A choice is when you pick one thing. Good choosers think first. They can say why they chose.
  show: flow stop > think > choose > say why
demo: It is cold outside. Shall I wear a coat or a T-shirt? I choose the coat, because it will keep me warm. "Because" gives the reason.
  show: chips I choose the coat | because | it keeps me warm
M: Match each choice to its reason.
  pairs: I wear a coat => Because it is cold | I drink water => Because I am thirsty | I go to bed => Because I am tired | I wash my hands => Because they are dirty
  hint: Why do you drink?
  why: A good choice has a reason. The reason comes after "because".
G: A safe choice, or not a safe choice?
  bin Safe: holding hands near the road | wearing a helmet on a bike | asking a grown-up for help
  bin Not safe: running into the road | touching a hot oven | climbing on a wobbly chair
  hint: Could you get hurt?
  why: Safe choices keep us from getting hurt.
P: It is raining. Which shoes do you choose?
  + Wellies, because they keep my feet dry :: A good choice, with a good reason.
  - Sandals, because I like them :: [reason-does-not-fit] Sandals will let the rain in.
  - Slippers, because they are soft :: [reason-does-not-fit] Slippers would get soaked.
  hint: What does the rain do to your feet?
  why: The best choice fits what is happening. In the rain, wellies keep feet dry.
>P: You can play outside or watch a show. The sun is out, and it will be dark soon. Which do you choose, and why?
  + Play outside now, because soon it will be dark :: The show can wait until later.
  - Watch the show, just because :: [no-reason] "Just because" is not a reason.
  - Do both at the same time :: [not-possible] You cannot be outside and in front of the show together.
  hint: Which one can you still do after dark?
  why: Thinking about what comes later helps us to choose well now.
done: Stop, think, choose, and say why. Tell someone the reason for a choice you made today.

@ mind.1.thinking
goal: Ask good questions, tell facts from opinions, and give reasons for what you believe.
intro: Good thinkers ask, "How do you know?" A fact is something that can be checked. An opinion is what someone thinks or feels. Both matter, but they are not the same.
  show: table Fact | Opinion ;; can be checked | what someone thinks or feels ;; A week has seven days. | Monday is the worst day.
demo: "It will rain today, because the clouds are dark." That is a claim with a reason. "It will rain today, because I want it to" is not a good reason. Wanting does not make something true.
  show: chips claim: it will rain | because | reason: the clouds are dark
G: Fact or opinion?
  bin Fact: A triangle has three sides. | Fish live in water. | A week has seven days.
  bin Opinion: Blue is the best colour. | Cats are nicer than dogs. | Peas taste horrible.
  hint: Could you check it and find out if it is true?
  why: Facts can be checked. Opinions are what people think or feel, and people can disagree about them.
B: Build a sentence that gives a reason.
  bank: I think | it will rain | because | the clouds are dark | I want it to
  answer: I think | it will rain | because | the clouds are dark
  x I want it to :: [wanting-is-not-a-reason] Wanting rain does not make it rain.
  hint: The reason comes after "because".
  why: A good reason is something that helps to show that the claim is true. Dark clouds often bring rain.
P: Tom says, "The moon is made of cheese, because my brother said so." Is that a good reason?
  + No: someone saying it does not make it true :: We need a way to check.
  - Yes: brothers are always right :: [trusted-without-checking] Anyone can be wrong. Ask how they know.
  - Yes: it is a fact because Tom said it :: [saying-makes-it-true] Saying something does not make it a fact.
  hint: How could we find out what the moon is made of?
  why: A good reason gives evidence. "Someone said so" is not enough.
>P: A friend tells you that there is a dragon in the park. What is a good question to ask?
  + "How do you know?" :: That asks for the reason.
  - "What colour is the dragon?" :: [assumed-it-is-true] That takes it as true before you have checked.
  - Nothing: just believe it :: [believed-without-checking] Good thinkers ask before they believe.
  hint: What do good thinkers ask?
  why: Before believing something surprising, ask for the reason or the evidence.
done: Ask "How do you know?" Use the word "because" three times today.

@ mind.1.friendship
goal: Be a good friend, work with others and sort out a quarrel fairly.
intro: Friends listen, share and help each other. Friends sometimes disagree, and that is normal. What matters is how they sort it out.
  show: chips listen | share | help | say sorry
demo: Two friends both want the red pen. They stop. Each says what they want. They think of ideas. They decide: "You use it first, then me." Both are happy with that.
  show: flow stop > say what you want > listen > think of ideas > choose a fair one
O: Put the steps for sorting out a quarrel in order.
  items: Stop and calm down | Say how you feel | Listen to the other person | Think of ideas together | Choose one that is fair to both
  ends: First | Last
  hint: It is hard to talk well when you are still cross.
  why: Calm down, speak, listen, think, choose. Each step makes the next one easier.
G: Does it help a friendship, or hurt it?
  bin Helps: listening | sharing | saying sorry
  bin Hurts: teasing | leaving someone out | breaking a promise
  hint: How would it make your friend feel?
  why: Friendships grow when we are kind, and are hurt when we are unkind.
P: You bump into a friend by accident, and they fall over. What should you do?
  + Say sorry and help them up :: It was an accident, but they are still hurt.
  - Say nothing, because it was an accident :: [accident-means-no-sorry] We say sorry for accidents too.
  - Laugh :: [unkind-choice] Laughing would hurt their feelings as well.
  hint: How would you want your friend to act if you were the one who fell?
  why: Saying sorry shows that you care about how your friend feels.
>P: Your friend wants to play football. You want to draw. What is a fair way to sort it out?
  + Do one first and then the other :: Both of you get what you want.
  - Make your friend do what you want :: [one-side-wins] That is fair to only one of you.
  - Stop being friends :: [gave-up] One small disagreement need not end a friendship.
  hint: Can you find a way for both of you to be happy?
  why: A compromise gives each person part of what they want.
done: Listen, share, say sorry. Sort out your next disagreement with these steps.

@ mind.1.money-sense
goal: Know the four things you can do with money, and plan how to save for something.
intro: You can do four things with money. You can earn it, save it, spend it or give it. You cannot spend the same coin twice, so you have to choose.
  show: chips earn | save | spend | give
demo: Zara gets 2 coins each week. She wants a kite that costs 6 coins. She saves her coins and does not spend them. After 3 weeks she has 6 coins, and she buys the kite.
  show: bars Week_1=2 Week_2=4 Week_3=6 unit=coins
M: Match each word to what it means.
  pairs: Earn => Get money for doing a job | Save => Keep money for later | Spend => Use money to buy something | Give => Use money to help someone else
  hint: Which word means keeping it?
  why: Earning, saving, spending and giving are the four things you can do with money.
F: Work out what is left.
  frame: You have 10 coins. You spend 4. You have __ left
  taps: 4 | 6 | 14
  answer: 6
  x 14 :: [added-instead] Spending takes coins away. 10 take away 4.
  x 4 :: [gave-the-amount-spent] 4 is what you spent. What is left?
  hint: 10 take away 4.
  why: 10 − 4 = 6. Money that is spent is gone.
G: A need or a want?
  bin Need: food | water | a warm coat
  bin Want: sweets | a new toy | a video game
  hint: Could you live without it?
  why: Needs are things we must have. Wants are nice to have, and they come after the needs.
>P: You get 2 coins every week. A toy costs 10 coins. How many weeks must you save?
  + 5 weeks :: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.
  - 2 weeks :: [gave-the-weekly-amount] 2 is how many coins you get each week.
  - 10 weeks :: [gave-the-price] 10 is the price. You get 2 coins a week.
  keep
  hint: Count up in twos until you reach 10.
  why: Five lots of 2 make 10. Saving a little each week adds up.
done: Earn, save, spend, give. Choose something to save for, and count how many weeks it will take.

@ mind.1.rules
goal: Explain why rules help, and work out what is fair.
intro: Rules help people to live and play together. A good rule keeps people safe or makes things fair. It is the same for everyone.
  show: chips safe | fair | the same for everyone
demo: At the slide, the rule is "wait in the queue". Without the rule, the pushiest children would have every turn. With the rule, everyone gets a turn.
  show: flow wait in the queue > have your turn > go to the back
M: Match each rule to the reason for it.
  pairs: Walk in the corridor => So that nobody gets hurt | Put your hand up => So that everyone can be heard | Wash your hands => So that germs do not spread | Put the toys away => So that nobody trips over them
  hint: Which rule is about germs?
  why: Every good rule has a reason. Knowing the reason helps us to keep the rule.
G: Fair or not fair?
  bin Fair: everyone has a turn | the same rules for everybody | owning up to a mistake
  bin Not fair: pushing in | changing the rules so that you win | taking more than your share
  hint: Would you think it fair if someone did it to you?
  why: Fair means that the same rules apply to everyone.
P: You find a coin on the playground. It is not yours. What is the right thing to do?
  + Hand it to a teacher :: Then whoever lost it can get it back.
  - Keep it, because nobody saw :: [nobody-saw] It still belongs to someone else.
  - Throw it away :: [wasted-it] Then the owner can never get it back.
  hint: How would you feel if you had lost the coin?
  why: Doing the right thing means thinking about the person who lost it.
>P: In a game you step over the line. Nobody sees. What is the right thing to do?
  + Own up :: Playing fair means keeping the rules even when nobody is watching.
  - Say nothing, because nobody saw :: [nobody-saw] The rule is the same whether or not anyone sees.
  - Blame someone else :: [blamed-another] That would be unfair to them.
  hint: Is the rule only for when people are watching?
  why: Being fair is about what you do, not about who is watching.
done: Rules keep things safe and fair. Find one rule at home and say what it is for.

@ mind.2.logic-intro
goal: Tell a valid argument from a faulty one, and work out what follows from what you know.
intro: An argument gives reasons, called premises, for a conclusion. In a valid argument, if the premises are true then the conclusion must be true as well. Logic is the study of which arguments work.
  show: steps All dogs are animals. ;; Rex is a dog. ;; So Rex is an animal.
demo: "All dogs are animals. Rex is a dog. So Rex is an animal." This is valid. Now try: "All dogs are animals. Tibby is an animal. So Tibby is a dog." That does not follow, because Tibby could be a cat. Being an animal is not enough to make something a dog.
  show: table Argument | Does it follow? ;; Rex is a dog, so Rex is an animal | yes ;; Tibby is an animal, so Tibby is a dog | no
P: All birds have feathers. A robin is a bird. What follows?
  + A robin has feathers :: It must be so, if both statements are true.
  - A robin can sing :: [added-outside-knowledge] That may be true, but it does not follow from these two statements.
  - All feathered things are robins :: [reversed-the-rule] The rule runs from birds to feathers, not back again.
  hint: Use only what the two statements tell you.
  why: If every bird has feathers and a robin is a bird, then a robin has feathers. Nothing else is guaranteed.
E: This argument does not work. Repair it by changing two lines.
  tokens: All cats are mammals. | Tibby is a mammal.{Tibby is a cat.} | So Tibby is a cat.{So Tibby is a mammal.}
  limit: 2
  aim: Change two lines so that the conclusion really follows.
  accept: 2=Tibby is a cat.&3=So Tibby is a mammal.
  hint: The rule runs from cats to mammals. Start with a cat.
  why: "All cats are mammals. Tibby is a cat. So Tibby is a mammal." Now the rule is used in the direction in which it runs.
G: Sound reasoning or faulty reasoning?
  bin Sound: All squares have four sides. This is a square. So it has four sides. | If it rains, the match is off. It is raining. So the match is off.
  bin Faulty: Everyone says it, so it must be true. | You are only a child, so you are wrong. | I wore my lucky socks and we won, so the socks made us win.
  hint: Does the conclusion have to be true if the reasons are true?
  why: Popularity, the age of the speaker and things that merely happened together are not good reasons.
K: Ana, Ben and Cy each own one pet: a cat, a dog or a fish. Turn over as few clues as you need to work out who owns the fish.
  clue One: Ben owns the dog.
  clue Two: Cy does not own the fish.
  clue Three: Ana is the tallest.
  clue Four: Ana owns the fish.
  need: 4 | 1,2
  + Ana :: She is the only one left for the fish.
  - Ben :: [ignored-a-clue] Ben owns the dog.
  - Cy :: [ignored-a-clue] Cy does not own the fish.
  hint: One clue settles it alone. Two others settle it together.
  why: Clue four tells you directly. Or use clues one and two: Ben has the dog and Cy does not have the fish, so Cy has the cat and Ana has the fish.
>P: If a shape is a square, then it has four sides. This shape has four sides. Is it a square?
  + Not necessarily: it could be a rectangle :: Four sides are needed, but they are not enough.
  - Yes, it must be a square :: [reversed-the-rule] The rule runs from squares to four sides, not back again.
  - No, it cannot be a square :: [overcorrection] It might be a square. We just cannot be sure.
  hint: Think of another shape with four sides.
  why: "If A then B" does not mean "if B then A". Running a rule backwards is one of the commonest mistakes in reasoning.
done: Check that the conclusion really follows. Find one "so" in an advertisement and test it.

@ mind.2.psychology-intro
goal: Explain how attention and memory work, and use that knowledge to learn better.
intro: Your mind takes in far more than it keeps. Attention chooses what gets in. Short-term memory holds a few things for a few seconds. Only what you practise and connect with what you know reaches long-term memory.
  show: flow notice > hold briefly > practise and connect > store > recall
demo: Try to remember 1 9 6 9 2 0 2 5 as eight separate digits. It is hard. Now see it as two years: 1969 and 2025. It is easy. Grouping things into meaningful chunks lets short-term memory hold more.
  show: table As single digits | As chunks ;; 1 9 6 9 2 0 2 5 | 1969 and 2025 ;; eight things to hold | two things to hold
O: Put the stages of remembering in order.
  items: Pay attention | Hold it in short-term memory | Practise it and connect it with what you know | Store it in long-term memory | Recall it when you need it
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You cannot remember what you never noticed.
  why: Attention comes first. Without practice and connection, most of what we notice fades in seconds.
P: Why is 1969-2025 easier to remember than 1 9 6 9 2 0 2 5?
  + It is two meaningful chunks, not eight separate things :: Short-term memory holds only a few items.
  - It has fewer digits :: [miscounted] The digits are exactly the same.
  - Years are always easy to remember :: [overgeneralised] It works because the digits were grouped into units that mean something to you.
  hint: How many things do you have to hold in each case?
  why: Chunking reduces the number of items. The limit is on the number of chunks, not on the number of digits.
G: Does it help memory, or hinder it?
  bin Helps: testing yourself | spreading practice over several days | linking new facts to what you know | a good night's sleep
  bin Hinders: studying with message alerts on | leaving everything to the night before | only rereading
  hint: Which ones make your brain work to bring the answer back?
  why: Memory grows stronger when you retrieve, space out your practice and make connections. Distraction and cramming weaken it.
P: You do your homework while answering messages. What happens?
  + You are slower and make more mistakes: attention has to keep switching :: The mind cannot do two demanding things at once.
  - You finish both faster :: [multitasking-myth] Each switch costs time and attention.
  - It makes no difference :: [ignored-the-cost] Switching has a cost every time.
  hint: Can you really read two things at the same moment?
  why: What feels like doing two things at once is rapid switching, and every switch loses something.
>P: You have 20 spellings to learn by Friday. Which plan works best?
  + A little each day: cover the word, write it, check it :: Spaced practice with self-testing.
  - Read the list twenty times on Thursday night :: [cramming-and-rereading] Rereading feels like learning, but little of it lasts.
  - Write each word once and hope :: [too-little-practice] Once is not enough to store it.
  hint: Which plan makes you bring the words back from memory on several different days?
  why: Spreading practice out and testing yourself are the two most reliable ways to make learning last.
done: Attend, chunk, space it out and test yourself. Use the plan on something you need to learn this week.

@ mind.2.society
goal: Describe what cultures and communities are, and meet differences between people with curiosity and not with judgement.
intro: A society is a large group of people who live together under shared arrangements. Within it people form communities and share cultures: ways of speaking, eating, celebrating and believing that are passed on from one generation to the next.
  show: table Word | Meaning ;; culture | a shared way of life ;; community | people who belong together ;; tradition | a custom passed down ;; norm | an unwritten rule about how to behave
demo: In some countries people greet each other by bowing. In others they shake hands, and in others they press their palms together. The customs differ, but the purpose is the same: to show respect when people meet.
  show: chips a bow | a handshake | palms together
M: Match each word to its meaning.
  pairs: Culture => A shared way of life | Community => People who belong together | Tradition => A custom passed down the generations | Norm => An unwritten rule about how to behave | Diversity => A variety of people and ways of life
  hint: A norm is a rule that nobody has written down.
  why: These words help us to describe how people live together.
G: Does it vary between cultures, or is it found in every human society?
  bin Varies between cultures: how people greet each other | the food eaten at celebrations | what people wear
  bin Found in every society: language | music | caring for children | ideas about fairness
  hint: Every society has music. Does every society have the same music?
  why: The forms differ, but people everywhere speak, make music, raise children and care about fairness.
C: Two ways of reacting to an unfamiliar custom. Which is the better starting point?
  left: text Their way is strange and wrong.
  right: text Their way is different from mine. I wonder why they do it.
  + The right: it begins with curiosity, and does not judge by one's own habits :: Understanding comes before judging.
  - The left: your own customs are the correct ones :: [own-way-is-the-standard] Everyone's customs look unusual from somewhere else.
  - Neither: it is better not to notice differences :: [avoidance] Noticing differences is fine. What matters is how we respond.
  hint: Which reaction would help you to learn something?
  why: Our own customs feel natural only because we grew up with them. Asking "why?" is how understanding starts.
>P: A new pupil brings food for lunch that the others have never seen, and some of them laugh. What is the best response?
  + Ask about it with interest: different is not wrong :: Every family's food would look unusual to someone.
  - Join in the laughing :: [went-along-with-the-group] It would hurt the new pupil, and there is nothing to laugh at.
  - Tell the new pupil to bring the same food as everyone else :: [demanded-sameness] People need not be the same in order to belong.
  hint: How would your own lunch look in another country?
  why: Meeting differences with curiosity makes a community that people can belong to as they are.
done: Different is not wrong. Find out about one tradition from a culture other than your own.

@ mind.2.big-questions
goal: Recognise a philosophical question, give reasons for an answer, and test an idea with examples.
intro: Some questions cannot be answered by looking them up or by measuring. What is fair? What is real? What makes a life good? Philosophy tackles these by careful thinking: giving reasons, and testing them.
  show: table Can be looked up or measured | Needs thinking and reasons ;; How tall is Everest? | Is it ever right to lie? ;; How many legs has a spider? | What makes someone a good friend?
demo: A wooden ship is repaired, one plank at a time, until after many years not one of the original planks is left. Is it still the same ship? There are good reasons to say yes and good reasons to say no. A question of that kind is a philosophical one.
  show: flow one plank is replaced > then another > at last every plank is new > is it the same ship?
G: What kind of question is it?
  bin Can be looked up or measured: How tall is Everest? | How many legs has a spider? | What is the capital of Peru?
  bin Needs thinking and reasons: Is it ever right to lie? | What makes someone a good friend? | Could a robot be a person?
  hint: Could a book or a ruler settle it?
  why: Questions of fact are settled by looking or measuring. Philosophical questions are settled, if at all, by reasons.
P: What makes the puzzle of the ship a philosophical one?
  + Good reasons point both ways: it has the same history, but none of the same material :: Facts alone cannot settle it.
  - The answer is in an encyclopedia :: [fact-question] We already know every fact. The question is what "the same" means.
  - It is a silly question :: [dismissed-it] The same puzzle arises for people, whose bodies also change completely over time.
  hint: Do we lack any facts about the ship?
  why: When all the facts are known and the question still stands, the question is about our ideas: here, the idea of being the same thing.
B: Build an answer with a reason and an exception.
  bank: I think that lying is usually wrong, | because people need to be able to trust one another, | but a lie that protects someone from harm may be an exception. | because I said so. | and everyone knows that.
  answer: I think that lying is usually wrong, | because people need to be able to trust one another, | but a lie that protects someone from harm may be an exception.
  x because I said so. :: [no-reason-given] Saying so is not a reason. A reason tells us why.
  x and everyone knows that. :: [appeal-to-everyone] What everyone supposedly knows still needs a reason.
  hint: A claim, then a reason, then a case that tests it.
  why: A good philosophical answer states a view, supports it and considers where it might not hold.
>P: A friend says, "Being happy means having lots of things." Which reply does the best philosophical work?
  + "Can you think of someone with lots of things who is unhappy? What would that show?" :: An example can test the claim.
  - "You are wrong." :: [bare-assertion] That states a view and gives no reason.
  - "My dad says that is not true." :: [appeal-to-authority] Someone saying so is not an argument.
  hint: Which reply tests the idea against a case?
  why: Philosophers test a general claim by looking for an example that does not fit. If there is one, the claim needs changing.
done: Give a reason, then test it. Choose one big question and write your answer in three sentences.

@ mind.2.study-skills
goal: Study in ways that make learning last: focus, notes in your own words, spaced practice and self-testing.
intro: How you study matters more than how long. The methods that feel easiest, rereading and highlighting, work least well. The methods that feel like effort work best.
  show: table Feels easy, works poorly | Feels hard, works well ;; rereading | testing yourself ;; highlighting | explaining it aloud ;; cramming | spreading practice over days
demo: Two pupils study the same page for the same length of time. One reads it four times. The other reads it once, closes the book and writes down everything she can remember, and then checks. A week later the second pupil remembers far more.
  show: flow read once > close the book > write what you remember > check
G: Active study or passive study?
  bin Active: testing yourself with flashcards | explaining it aloud to someone | doing practice questions
  bin Passive: rereading the page | highlighting almost everything | copying notes word for word
  hint: Does it make you bring the information back from memory?
  why: Active methods make you retrieve and use what you know. Passive methods only show it to you again.
O: Put the parts of a good study session in order.
  items: Put your phone away and set one goal | Study for about 25 minutes | Test yourself without looking | Check your answers and fix the mistakes | Take a short break
  ends: First | Last
  hint: Test yourself before you check.
  why: A clear goal, a spell of focus, retrieval, correction and rest. The testing and the correcting are where most of the learning happens.
P: Which way of taking notes helps you to learn most?
  + Writing the main ideas in your own words :: You have to understand an idea to rephrase it.
  - Copying every word exactly :: [copying-without-thinking] You can copy without understanding a thing.
  - Taking no notes and trusting your memory :: [overconfidence] Most of what we hear once is forgotten within days.
  hint: Which one makes you think about what it means?
  why: Putting ideas into your own words forces you to process them, and that is what makes them stick.
P: You have a test in a week. Which timetable is better?
  + Twenty minutes on each of six days :: Spaced practice.
  - Two hours on the night before :: [cramming] The same total time, crammed, gives far less lasting memory.
  hint: The total time is the same. What differs?
  why: Returning to material after a gap, when you have begun to forget it, strengthens the memory much more than one long session.
>P: You have read a chapter and you feel that you know it. What is the best way to check?
  + Close the book and write or say what you remember :: Only retrieval shows what you can really recall.
  - Read it once more :: [familiarity-as-knowledge] Rereading makes the page look familiar, which is not the same as being able to recall it.
  - Highlight more of it :: [marking-as-learning] Highlighting marks the text. It does not test your memory.
  hint: The feeling of knowing can mislead. What would actually test it?
  why: Recognising something is easier than recalling it, so material can feel known when it is not. Testing yourself reveals the difference.
done: Retrieve, space it out and use your own words. Try one self-test today on something you studied yesterday.
